			<summary>What is being measured?</summary>
			<dl>
				<dt>Adult citizens (CPS)</dt><dd>Civilian noninstitutionalized adults who are citizens.
					This is not a count of the legally eligible population. Turnout is self-reported.</dd>
				<dt>All adult residents (CPS)</dt><dd>The same survey population, including noncitizens.
					Do not compare this denominator directly with citizen turnout.</dd>
				<dt>NC registered voters</dt><dd>Historical voter-history participants divided by election-time
					active and inactive registrations. Voter-history totals differ from official ballots cast;
					the reconciliation download preserves both.</dd>
				<dt>Administrative local participation</dt><dd>EAC-reported participants divided by reported total
					registrations, with source-specific active/inactive rules. These are county, municipal or statewide
					reporting offices, not uniformly precincts or an eligible-population denominator.
					Counts extend to 2004–2024. The 2004/2006 harmonized totals count ballots counted,
					while later cast-ballot definitions differ. Unmatched boundaries and missing denominators
					are not filled; no 2004/2006 election-year map is substituted.</dd>
			</dl>
			<p>National observations extend to 1964 and state observations to 1972, where supported.
				Citizen-based education and sex estimates cover 2000–2024; older education and sex
				series use all adult residents. Race definitions, citizenship measurement, and
				voting-age rules change across the historical series. Chart lines break at the documented transitions;
				<a href="https://www.census.gov/topics/public-sector/voting/about/faqs.html">consistent citizenship measurement</a>
				begins in 1994, so 1994 and 1996 remain connected.</p>
			<p>NC precinct counts cover 2002–2024. The available election-year map snapshots are
				2006, 2012, 2014, 2016, 2020, 2022, and 2024. Other years do not borrow newer maps.
				Matching a precinct ID is not independent certification of election-day boundaries
				or proof that a precinct stayed unchanged. NC education is not available.</p>
			<p>Missing participation, inconsistent counts, and zero denominators have no displayed rate.
				Unmatched records stay in the downloads. Outside the explicitly listed precinct sources,
				additional precinct inputs remain unverified, not declared unavailable. State-level CPS estimates
				do not fill those gaps.</p>
			<div id="methods"></div>
		</details>
		{methodsPointer && <details id="method-reference" class="participation-reference">
			<summary>National voting methods, 1996–2024 · complete survey history</summary>
			<p>CPS shares among voters, not turnout rates among eligible citizens. The 1996–2016 series excludes
				unknown method/timing responses; 2018–2024 retains them. The questionnaire also changed in 2004.
				State administrative reporting starts in 2008 and may combine absentee methods or omit categories.</p>
			<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Percent of voters by method; definitions differ across the 2018 break.</caption>
				<thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Election Day · in person</th><th>Early · in person</th><th>Mail</th><th>Unknown</th><th>Survey universe</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>{methodOverall.map(row => <tr><th scope="row">{row.year}</th>
					{['election_day', 'early_in_person', 'mail', 'unknown'].map(mode =>
						<td>{row.methods[mode] == null ? 'Excluded / not reported' : `${row.methods[mode]!.toFixed(1)}%`}</td>)}
					<td>{row.universe.replaceAll('_', ' ')}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
			</table></div>
		</details>}
		</section>
